<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kimweri ye Nyumbai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1862">1862</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kimweri ye Nyumbai was king of the Shambaa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usambara-mountains">Usambara Mountains</a> of north-eastern Tanzania during the first half of the 19th century. Under him the Kilindi kingdom reached its greatest extent, ruling the mountains through sons placed as chiefs and receiving tribute from the coastal plains. He received the missionaries Krapf and Erhardt at his capital, Vugha.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kimweri-ye-nyumbai.jpg" alt="Kimweri ye Nyumbai" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser lightly adapted by Aymatth2 (to identify Usambara Mountains). Elevation data from SRTM, all other features from Natural Earth. … · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Usambara_Mountains_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kimweri belonged to the Kilindi dynasty, founded by his great-grandfather Mbegha, a hunter who according to legend was cast out by the Nguu and won the acceptance of the Shambaa as ruler of their capital. Kilindi rulers bore the title Simbe Mwene, the Lion King. Mbegha was followed by his son Bughe and then by Bughe's son Kinyashi, Kimweri's father, who strengthened the kingdom against slavers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raiders and conquered the Zigua country in the lower Pangani valley and the eastern Usambara mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>By 1835 Kimweri controlled the whole coast below the Usambaras, and his realm reached from the mountains to the shore between Tanga and Pangani and into the plains to the south and east. He was said to have 300 wives, and he placed his many sons as chiefs in most districts; the kingdom was relatively peaceful, since he could overrule their unjust decisions. The newly subjected east was ruled more harshly, and witches, rebels and prisoners of war could be sold to coastal traders, usually for arms and ammunition.</p><p>The missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-krapf">Johann Ludwig Krapf</a>, who came in 1848 and 1852, found him hospitable and an effective ruler, and in 1852 watched tribute from the coast being shared out at Vugha: of 200 pieces of cloth Kimweri took 100. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakob-erhardt">Johann Jakob Erhardt</a> saw two accused witches executed there in September 1853. That year the growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> led to conflict with Seyyid Said, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar; the two agreed on a form of joint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the coast, and Kimweri took the title of Sultan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Richard Burton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a> visited in 1857, Burton found an old man emaciated by sickness. After Kimweri's death in 1862 a civil war over the succession broke out, fed by competition for the wealth of the caravan trade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and slave trading broke the kingdom apart; in 1898 fire destroyed Vugha. In later memory his reign was seen as a golden age of peace, when the king treated all his people as his children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kimweri ye Nyumbai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Kimweri ye Nyumbai</b>, who died in 1862, the kingdom reached its height, controlling the mountains and receiving tribute from the coastal plains.</blockquote><a href="/state/shambaa-kingdom">Shambaa Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
